<!DOCTYPE html><html xmlns:o="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office" xmlns:w="urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:word" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/TR/REC-html40"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><title>analytic-geometry</title></head><body><h1>analytic-geometry</h1><h2>Table of Contents</h2><ul><li>Straight line: length of perpendicular from a given point…</li><li>Angle Bisector — Worked Example (a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ method)</li><li>Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree…</li><li>Angle Bisector — Verifying with the tan θ Method</li><li>Coordinates in space: points in space, distance between t…</li><li>Geometry 9-1</li></ul><h2>Straight line: length of perpendicular from a given point…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Straight line: length of perpendicular from a given point… — Analytic Geometry, Mathematics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: straight line, length of perpendicular from a given point to a given line, bisectors of the angles between two straight lines · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Line: point–slope y − y₁ = m(x − x₁); perpendicular distance from (x₁,y₁) to ax+by+c=0 is |ax₁+by₁+c|/√(a²+b²).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Pair of lines: a·S ≡ ax² + 2hxy + by²; represents a real pair iff h² ≥ ab. Angle between: tanθ = 2√(h²−ab)/(a+b).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Homogeneous: ax² + 2hxy + by² = 0 (two lines through origin); bisectors: (x² − y²)/(a−b) = xy/h.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Fact.** Direction cosines of a line: l² + m² + n² = 1; distance between two points in space √(Δx²+Δy²+Δz²).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Length of perpendicular from a given point to a given line.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Bisectors of the angles between two straight lines.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Straight line</td></tr><tr><td>• Length of perpendicular from a given point to a given line</td></tr><tr><td>• Bisectors of the angles between two straight lines</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Straight line" as one blob → ✅ split into: straight line, length of perpendicular from a given point to a given line, bisectors of the angles between two straight lines.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ answer with no steps → ✅ show each step and box the result.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ line: point–slope y − y₁ = m(x − x₁); perpendicular distance from (x₁,y₁) to ax+by+c=0 is |ax₁+by₁+c|/√(a²+b²)..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Straight line: length of perpendicular from a given point… (Analytic Geometry, Mathematics, class-11-notes) covers Straight line, length of perpendicular from a given point to a given line, bisectors of the angles between two straight lines. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Angle Bisector — Worked Example (a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ method)</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Bisector equations.** The angle bisectors of the lines a₁x + b₁y + c₁ = 0 and a₂x + b₂y + c₂ = 0 are (a₁x + b₁y + c₁)/√(a₁² + b₁²) = ± (a₂x + b₂y + c₂)/√(a₂² + b₂²).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Equal distance property.** Any point on a bisector is equidistant from the two lines, using the perpendicular distance |ax + by + c|/√(a² + b²).</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Acute vs obtuse.** The two bisectors are perpendicular; when a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ < 0 the '+' combination bisects the acute angle, and when a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ > 0 it bisects the obtuse angle.</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Worked steps.** Write both equations in general form, divide each by the square root of the sum of its squared coefficients, combine with ±, and simplify each result.</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Use of the result.** The bisector equations locate the in-centre of a triangle (intersection of internal bisectors) and solve locus problems requiring equal distance from two lines.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Angle Bisector — Worked Example (a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ method) to other topics</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Angle Bisector Between Two Lines — The a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ Rule to other topics</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angle Bisector — Worked Example (a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ method) covers essential principles and applications. Angle Bisector Between Two Lines — The a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ Rule covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>**Scope.** Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree… — Analytic Geometry, Mathematics (class-11-notes).</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**Tested.** define it · list: pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree in x, condition for representing a pair of lines, homogenous second-degree equation in x, angle between pair of lines · one worked example · one misconception each.</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**Fact.** Line: point–slope y − y₁ = m(x − x₁); perpendicular distance from (x₁,y₁) to ax+by+c=0 is |ax₁+by₁+c|/√(a²+b²).</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Fact.** Pair of lines: a·S ≡ ax² + 2hxy + by²; represents a real pair iff h² ≥ ab. Angle between: tanθ = 2√(h²−ab)/(a+b).</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>**Fact.** Homogeneous: ax² + 2hxy + by² = 0 (two lines through origin); bisectors: (x² − y²)/(a−b) = xy/h.</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>**Fact.** Direction cosines of a line: l² + m² + n² = 1; distance between two points in space √(Δx²+Δy²+Δz²).</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>**Condition for representing a pair of lines.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>**Homogenous second-degree equation in x.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>**Angle between pair of lines.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>**Bisectors of the angles between pair of lines.** one-line definition + one-line exam use.</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree in x</td></tr><tr><td>• Condition for representing a pair of lines</td></tr><tr><td>• Homogenous second-degree equation in x</td></tr><tr><td>• Angle between pair of lines</td></tr><tr><td>• Bisectors of the angles between pair of lines</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ treat "Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree in x" as one blob → ✅ split into: pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree in x, condition for representing a pair of lines, homogenous second-degree equation in x.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ state the rule without its limit → ✅ note when it applies / stops applying.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ answer with no steps → ✅ show each step and box the result.</td></tr><tr><td>❌ skip the key fact → ✅ line: point–slope y − y₁ = m(x − x₁); perpendicular distance from (x₁,y₁) to ax+by+c=0 is |ax₁+by₁+c|/√(a²+b²)..</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree… (Analytic Geometry, Mathematics, class-11-notes) covers Pair of straight lines: general equation of second degree in x, condition for representing a pair of lines, homogenous second-degree equation in x. Master the standard form, the sub-ideas and one worked example; most exam questions on this topic are built from exactly these.</p><hr><h2>Angle Bisector — Verifying with the tan θ Method</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td>As a cross-check, find the angle θ between one of the original lines and one candidate bisector using: tan θ = |(m₁ − m_b)/(1 + m₁·m_b)|, where m₁ is the original line's slope and m_b is the bisector's slope</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>**If tan θ < 1** (θ < 45°): since θ is half the angle between the two original lines, the total angle is less than 90°, so this bisector is the **acute** angle bisector</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>**If tan θ > 1** (θ > 45°): this bisector is the **obtuse** angle bisector</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>**Worked check:** slope of Bisector 1 (6x + 6y − 1 = 0) is m_b = −6/6 = −1; with the original line's slope m₁ = −1/2: tan θ = |(−1/2 − (−1))/(1 + (−1/2)(−1))| = |(1/2)/(3/2)| = 1/3</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Since tan θ = 1/3 < 1, this confirms 6x + 6y − 1 = 0 is definitively the **acute angle bisector** — matching the a₁a₂ + b₁b₂ method</td></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Connection of Angle Bisector — Verifying with the tan θ Method to other topics</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Angle Bisector — Verifying with the tan θ Method covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Coordinates in space: points in space, distance between t…</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>• Coordinates in space: points in space</td></tr><tr><td>• Distance between two points</td></tr><tr><td>• Direction cosines</td></tr><tr><td>• Ratios of a line</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Coordinates in Space to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr><tr><td>❌ answer with no steps → ✅ show each step and box the result.</td></tr></table><p><b>Summary:</b> Coordinates in Space covers essential principles and applications.</p><hr><h2>Geometry 9-1</h2><table><tr><th>#</th><th>Concept</th></tr></table><h3>Key Points</h3><table><tr><th>Point</th></tr><tr><td>Application of Geometry 9-1</td></tr><tr><td>Connection of Geometry 9-1 to other topics</td></tr></table><h3>Common Confusions</h3><table><tr><th>❌ / ✅</th></tr></table>